Project Structure & Coding Standards

MVVM-based Modular Structure

Each feature/module should follow:

/app
  /[feature-name]
    /components     → UI Components
    /views          → Pages/screens
    /view-models    → Business logic and interactions
    /services       → Axios-based API handlers
    /types          → Feature-specific TypeScript types
    /hooks          → Feature-specific hooks
/config
  global-config.ts   → App-wide settings (app name, description, etc.)
/styles
  globals.css        → Central theming and global styles
/lib                 → Shared utilities (formatters, helpers)

	•	Follow MVVM pattern strictly with a clean modular breakdown.
	•	Keep each module self-contained, importing only from /lib or /config when needed.

⸻

Global Styling & Theming
	•	All theming, font styles, utility classes, and responsive design rules must be defined in globals.css inside /styles.
	•	Avoid inline styling or scoped styles unless absolutely necessary.
	•	Update globals.css for:
	•	Colors
	•	Typography
	•	Animations
	•	Layout utilities
	•	Media queries

Always reference globals.css for consistent UI across the entire project.

⸻

⚙️ Global Configuration
	•	Use global-config.ts (inside /config) for app-wide configurations such as:
	•	App name
	•	Default descriptions
	•	Page titles & SEO metadata
	•	API base URLs (if not environment-dependent)
	•	Misc flags like feature toggles or environment modes

 No hardcoded strings or config values inside individual modules. Pull everything from global-config.ts.

⸻

Best Practices
	•	Follow DRY, KISS, and SOLID principles
	•	Use shared components from a common module when reuse is possible
	•	Maintain strict naming conventions:
	•	PascalCase → Components
	•	camelCase → Variables, functions
	•	kebab-case → Folders and file names (except components/hooks/types)
	•	Prettier + ESLint are required (use team config)
	•	Define and use TypeScript types/interfaces consistently
	•	Always split logic out of components into view-models or services

⸻

Error Handling
	•	Centralize error handling via Axios interceptors (within /lib)
	•	Use try/catch in view-models for error-prone logic
	•	Return user-friendly messages, never raw errors
	•	Consider a reusable handleError function for consistency

⸻

